Poll from the browser. This platform can't hold a push connection, and the data only changes once a minute, so a 15-second poll shows each change within 15 seconds at trivial cost.

Push doesn't fit here:
- WebSocket handshakes on `api/` paths get a 501 at the edge.
- A server-sent events stream is one invocation, so the 10-second kill would cut it off and the browser would reconnect. The function would still have to poll the database to learn of the job's write, so nothing is saved.
- The only real push option is to have the rollup job publish to an external realtime service after each write, with the browser subscribing there. That adds a vendor for a once-a-minute dataset.

The change is confined to `public/dashboard.js`. `api/metrics.js` needs no change, and its `Cache-Control: no-store` stays so each poll reads fresh rows.

```js
let lastSeen = null;

async function load() {
  if (document.hidden) return;                 // no polling in background tabs
  const res = await fetch('/api/metrics');
  if (!res.ok) return;                         // keep showing the last good data
  const { rows, computed_at } = await res.json();
  if (!computed_at || computed_at === lastSeen) return;  // nothing new, or empty rollup
  lastSeen = computed_at;
  render(rows);
  document.getElementById('stamp').textContent = new Date(computed_at).toLocaleTimeString();
}

window.addEventListener('load', () => {
  load();
  setInterval(load, 15000);
});
```

- The 15-second interval is because the job can write at any point in a minute, so a 60-second poll could show a change up to a minute late. That is four small requests a minute per open tab.
- Skipping unchanged `computed_at` means the page re-renders only when the job has written. The client keeps `lastSeen` itself because instances share no memory.
- The refresh button can be removed, or left in place as a no-op when nothing has changed.
- `rollup.md` doesn't say whether the truncate-and-rewrite runs in one transaction. If it runs as separate statements, a poll can land on an empty table. The `!computed_at` guard covers that case, but the job should still write in a single transaction.

